      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "A 13-year-old male with a history of chronic congenital megacolon presented to the emergency department with a 1-day history of increasing abdominal pain, distension, and emesis. The patient was admitted for bowel disimpaction and irrigation. The patient rapidly developed an acute abdominal compartment syndrome because of his massive colonic dilation. Surgical decompression resulted in a reperfusion phenomenon and ultimately resulted in coagulopathy and patient demise. This case presents a unique cause of the abdominal compartment syndrome and discusses the implications to the emergency physician."},
